\title{A target without scalable vectors takes a fixed scale}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{simd}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-13}
\meta{completed}{2026-11-13}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-15}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted.}
\p{\strong{Context.} LLVM compiles a scalable vector only for a target that has them: AArch64 with SVE and RISC-V with the vector extension. The x86-64 backend and the AArch64 backend without SVE abort with \code{\verb~|Don't know how to legalize this scalable vector type~}; there is no fallback to a fixed vector. Checked with \code{\verb~|llc~} on LLVM 23.1. The first implementation therefore rejected a program that used a scalable vector on such a target, \pr{996}, which made scalable source unusable on most machines.}
\p{\strong{Decision.} The scale of a scalable vector is any positive constant of the machine, so a target without scalable vectors takes one. The pass \code{\verb~|reussir-fix-vector-scale~} runs before the lowering to LLVM on such a target: every scalable vector type becomes the fixed type of the same extents, \code{\verb~|vector.vscale~} becomes 1, and the operations that exist only for scalable vectors become their fixed counterparts. \pr{1008}.}
\p{\strong{Consequences.} One source runs everywhere; \code{\verb~|Simd<[u8; [16]]>~} is 16 lanes on x86-64 and NEON. The frontend, the language rules and the MIR stay independent of the target: a scalable vector is still only a parameter, a result or a local. The examples written with scalable vectors can be executed on the development machine. A wider fixed vector is not chosen automatically; a program that wants 32 or 64 lanes on AVX names a fixed type.}
\p{\strong{Revised 2026-11-15: the scale is a choice.} The first form of this decision fixed the scale at one. It is now an option of the pass, set by \code{\verb~|rrc --fallback-vscale <N>~}, and 4 when the option is absent, so \code{\verb~|Simd<[u8; [16]]>~} is 64 lanes on such a target: one AVX-512 register, two of AVX2, four of SSE2 or NEON. A scalable extent is multiplied by the scale, \code{\verb~|vector.vscale~} is that constant, and the position and the length of a scalable sub-vector count multiples of it. A target with scalable vectors ignores the option. \pr{1008}.}
\p{\strong{Why 4.} Two loops were timed on an x86-64 machine with fixed vectors of the size each scale gives, on long inputs, relative to a scale of one. A dot product of \code{\verb~|f32~} with masked loads and a vector accumulator runs 2.0, 4.0 and 5.9 times as fast at scales 2, 4 and 8 with AVX-512, and 2.0, 3.7 and 5.3 times with AVX2, because a wider accumulator is several independent ones; with SSE2 it gains a fifth at any scale. A count of a byte with masked loads gains 1.3, 1.6 and 1.5 with AVX-512, loses a quarter at scale 8 with SSE2, and runs at a third of the speed at every scale above one with AVX2, which has no masked load of bytes. On inputs shorter than one vector a larger scale costs: the count over 13 bytes runs at 0.86, 0.72 and 0.40 of the speed of scale one with AVX-512. Four takes most of the gain of the accumulating loop at half the short-input cost of eight. NEON was not measured.}
\p{\strong{Consequence for linking.} A scalable vector in a signature has a size that depends on the scale, so every package linked into one program must be compiled with the same one. Nothing checks that yet.}
